\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Quartic vertices on an alternating cycle}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{On the [alternating four-cycle](fdip-0003) with weights
#{(1,t,1,t)}, take #{t>0}, #{m>0} and #{s=1+t+m^2}. Retain
#{R=\{0,2\}}, eliminate #{U=\{1,3\}}, and choose the interaction
##{\mathcal V(\phi)=\frac{\phi_1^4+\phi_3^4}{24}.}
In retained coordinates #{x=(x_0,x_2)}, the
[conditional rules](fdip-0060) give independent eliminated noises
of variance #{1/s}, with means
##{\mu_1=\frac{x_0+tx_2}{s},\qquad
   \mu_3=\frac{tx_0+x_2}{s}.}
The first coefficient #{a(x)} of the fiber interaction is
##{\begin{aligned}
a(x)={}&\frac{(1+t^4)(x_0^4+x_2^4)}{24s^4}\\
 &+\frac{4t(1+t^2)(x_0^3x_2+x_0x_2^3)
                +12t^2x_0^2x_2^2}{24s^4}\\
 &+\frac{(1+t^2)(x_0^2+x_2^2)+4tx_0x_2}{4s^3}
   +\frac1{4s^2}.
\end{aligned}}
Thus eliminating a quartic supported on the removed vertices creates
quartic and quadratic couplings on the retained vertices, as well
as a constant.}

\proof{
\p{The eliminated precision is #{D=sI_2}; its cross block gives the
two means above. For a centered real Gaussian #{\eta} of variance
#{v}, Wick's identity gives #{\mathbb E\eta^4=3v^2}. Expanding
#{(\mu+\eta)^4} and discarding odd moments yields
##{\mathbb E(\mu+\eta)^4=\mu^4+6\mu^2v+3v^2.}
Consequently
##{a(x)=\frac{\mu_1^4+\mu_3^4}{24}
       +\frac{\mu_1^2+\mu_3^2}{4s}+\frac1{4s^2}.}
The binomial expansions of #{(x_0+tx_2)^4+(tx_0+x_2)^4} give
coefficients #{1+t^4}, #{4t(1+t^2)} and #{12t^2}. Squaring the
two numerators gives
#{(1+t^2)(x_0^2+x_2^2)+4tx_0x_2}. Substitution proves every
coefficient in the displayed polynomial.}
}

\p{In these named vertex coordinates, the ratio of the
#{x_0^3x_2} coefficient to the #{x_0^4} coefficient is
##{r(t)=\frac{4t(1+t^2)}{1+t^4}.}
It is unchanged by exchanging the two retained vertices and obeys
##{\begin{aligned}
r(t)&=r(1/t),&r(2)=r(1/2)&=\frac{40}{17},\\
r'(t)&=\frac{4(1-t^2)(1+4t^2+t^4)}{(1+t^4)^2}.
\end{aligned}}
The derivative is positive for #{0<t<1}. Therefore #{r} separates
the parameter for #{0<t\le1}, and on all positive parameters it separates
exactly modulo #{t\leftrightarrow1/t}. This is a vertex-coordinate
shape ratio, not an invariant of arbitrary orthogonal changes of the
retained basis. The complete coefficient tensors instead obey the
[naturality theorem](fdip-005Z).}

\p{The reciprocal equality resembles that of the
[trace ratio](fdip-000I), but neither equality gives a strict
fixed-mass background isomorphism at reciprocal parameters.
The edge-weight products are #{t^2} and #{t^{-2}}, so a
weight-preserving edge bijection would force #{t=1}. Also #{s}
changes at fixed mass; equality of the shape ratio does not make all
induced coefficients equal.}

\p{A finite numerical illustration uses #{t=2}, #{m=1} and
#{x=(0.5,-0.3)}. The means are #{(-1/40,7/40)}, and the exact
coefficient is
##{a(x)=\frac{541201}{30720000}=0.017617220052\ldots.}
One million independent conditional samples, using NumPy's PCG64
generator with seed #{20261031}, give #{0.0175836} with one estimated
standard error #{0.0000404}. The estimate concerns this one conditional
moment; the polynomial identity and exact value follow from the
analytic calculation above.}
